5 17592186045417 true] [17592186434503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434479 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434483 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434489 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434503 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434495 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434503 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434503 1077 "selection" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434503 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434495 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434503 1079 #uuid "bd9a0211-1803-541f-9d71-227b97979d9f" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434504 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434470 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434504 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434471 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434504 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434486 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434504 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434488 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434504 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434496 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434504 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434504 1077 "selection" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434504 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434496 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434504 1079 #uuid "d8fe19c0-cb1a-5326-b79f-39ab45804633" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434504 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434505 1069 "q-143551" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434505 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{11}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ is unknown and $\\sigma $ is known, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $max({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{11},\\mu )$\n\nII. $min({X}_{2},{X}_{4},…,{X}_{10},\\sigma )$\n\nIII. $\\frac{{X}_{1}+{X}_{11}}{{\\mu }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+1}$" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434505 1072 #uuid "673ddc27-8133-50ba-9995-aed461126acc" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434505 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{11}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434500 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434506 1069 "q-149835" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434506 1071 "Suppose the following data are sampled from a population.\n\n$$\n2.3,\\,2.6,\\,3.1,\\,2.8\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({X}_{i}+1)^{2}}{n-2}$ for this particular sample." 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434506 1072 #uuid "afcca6b7-f927-5420-8ae5-122ee674dc61" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434506 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}\\frac{({x}_{i}+1)^{2}}{4-2} & =\\frac{1}{2}((2.3+1)^{2}+(2.6+1)^{2}+(3.1+1)^{2}+(2.8+1)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(10.89+12.96+16.81+14.44) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(55.1) \\\\  & =27.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $27.55.$" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434503 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434507 1069 "q-139802" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434507 1071 "Suppose the following data are sampled from a population.\n\n$$\n7.3,\\,8.1,\\,6.9,\\,7.6,\\,8.5\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}$ for this particular sample." 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434507 1072 #uuid "d07ab9ad-d8e6-53a3-916d-ef13c3850d54" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434507 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} & =\\frac{1}{5}(7.3+8.1+6.9+7.6+8.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(38.4) \\\\  & =7.68.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $7.68.$" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434499 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434508 1069 "q-139833" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434508 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{X}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=6}^{10}{X}_{i}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\sqrt{{X}_{i}^{2}-{\\sigma }^{2}}$\n\nIII. $\\frac{{X}_{1}+{X}_{10}}{2}+\\mu $" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434508 1072 #uuid "d216f49f-dbd6-5da5-81b2-ff39a5445339" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434508 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434501 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434509 1069 "q-139837" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434509 1071 "The weights, in pounds, of six randomly selected newborns drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n5.5,\\,5.8,\\,6.2,\\,5.1,\\,6.4,\\,6\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\frac{n}{n+1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}$ for this particular sample." 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434509 1072 #uuid "e8084345-dfbb-5406-9137-a5e08b474106" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434509 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{6+1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} & =\\frac{6}{7}(5.5+5.8+6.2+5.1+6.4+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}(35) \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $30\\,\\text{lb}.$" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434502 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434510 1069 "q-143554" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434510 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both  known, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}|{X}_{i}-\\mu |$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}({X}_{i}+\\sigma )$\n\nIII. $min({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12})$" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434510 1072 #uuid "fd7fa324-de44-5c60-a60e-ad08ed2ad90d" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true] [17592186434510 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ and the *known* population mean $\\mu $ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only.\n\n- Expression III is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534735 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-05bb2d48-37d8-584c-872b-45fab789e483" 17592186434469 "answer-06448ce2-0e18-514f-b68e-1203b53623bf" 17592186434470 "answer-09c7d387-d49f-536a-b195-0974f7eff35f" 17592186434471 "answer-0e473785-f50e-53de-86c6-0a0d56d437c5" 17592186434472 "answer-1f42cb21-e60c-5d40-a114-5979a4cb5b1f" 17592186434473 "answer-22666824-84aa-5663-9208-faedd927de76" 17592186434474 "answer-23ef56c6-3700-5a51-a7ea-a3fb4bd0dc99" 17592186434475 "answer-29453521-97e6-5150-a7bc-602fae176d59" 17592186434476 "answer-2f80261e-f3fc-5e23-8e3f-a6acba9abcf8" 17592186434477 "answer-327520ab-534d-5508-b50a-db2d10472cde" 17592186434478 "answer-32a012c8-5672-50ff-9832-8858dc764753" 17592186434479 "answer-3cdaafcf-a37f-58e8-84d1-3d0c20ba5cfd" 17592186434480 "answer-3d10fc58-31b9-561f-8371-ffbba6ce6263" 17592186434481 "answer-471b22b4-5280-5ef4-b6a9-086940207f69" 17592186434482 "answer-4dd7ab18-c424-587e-b94f-4966d49cd86c" 17592186434483 "answer-52fdc8a2-4716-5414-bfb3-eb64b770f20c" 17592186434484 "answer-568b0d55-78f4-5d7c-b63a-bcab6a620e80" 17592186434485 "answer-5ba67d51-ba43-50e4-9513-6d222e3e8af9" 17592186434486 "answer-606a2a26-1a6c-57ef-b3cd-f1e192952714" 17592186434487 "answer-73623fce-c5b8-5155-ae34-d860b81c911a" 17592186434488 "answer-8dec3a1d-9535-5c8f-9c8f-05ed89cdf98a" 17592186434489 "answer-933c8972-9d57-57dd-8bc9-0b6bb925355e" 17592186434490 "answer-965e4b74-64fe-591d-9c4c-09d3918cbb1d" 17592186434491 "answer-97752404-ed02-56de-92db-254ae5